// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Geometry starts with three simple ideas that everything else is built on. Let's meet them!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Points, lines and planes are undefined terms: we describe them instead of defining them."},{"t":"list","items":["A point is an exact location with no size, named with a capital letter like A.","A line goes on forever in both directions and is named by two points, like line AB.","A plane is a flat surface that goes on forever, named by three points not on the same line."]},{"t":"p","text":"A segment is part of a line with two endpoints (segment AB). A ray starts at one endpoint and goes forever in one direction (ray AB starts at A)."},{"t":"key","text":"Two points determine exactly one line, and two lines intersect in at most one point."}]},{"say":"On the coordinate plane, we can find the middle of a segment and its exact length with two handy formulas.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The midpoint of a segment splits it into two equal parts. Average the coordinates:"},{"t":"key","text":"Midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂) ÷ 2, (y₁ + y₂) ÷ 2) and distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²)"},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the midpoint and length of the segment from (1, 2) to (7, 10)","steps":["Midpoint: ((1 + 7) ÷ 2, (2 + 10) ÷ 2) = (4, 6)","Distance: √(6² + 8²) = √100"],"answer":"midpoint (4, 6), length 10"},{"t":"p","text":"The distance formula is really the Pythagorean theorem: the horizontal and vertical changes are the legs of a right triangle."},{"t":"p","text":"Segment addition: if B is between A and C, then AB + BC = AC."}]},{"say":"Angles are everywhere in geometry. Let's learn how they're measured and the special pairs to watch for.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint, the vertex. Angles are measured in degrees."},{"t":"list","items":["Acute: less than 90°. Right: exactly 90°. Obtuse: between 90° and 180°. Straight: 180°.","Complementary angles add to 90°. Supplementary angles add to 180°.","Vertical angles are opposite each other when two lines cross. They are always congruent.","A linear pair is two adjacent angles that form a straight line, so they're supplementary."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Two angles are supplementary. One is 3 times the other. Find both.","steps":["x + 3x = 180","4x = 180, so x = 45"],"answer":"45° and 135°"}]}],[{"say":"Proofs are built from if-then statements. Let's learn how to flip them around and when they stay true.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A conditional statement has the form \"If p, then q.\" p is the hypothesis and q is the conclusion."},{"t":"list","items":["Converse: If q, then p.","Inverse: If not p, then not q.","Contrapositive: If not q, then not p."]},{"t":"key","text":"A conditional and its contrapositive are always both true or both false. The converse and inverse can differ."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Statement: If an angle measures 30°, then it is acute. Is the converse true?","steps":["Converse: If an angle is acute, then it measures 30°","A 50° angle is acute but not 30°: a counterexample"],"answer":"The converse is false"},{"t":"p","text":"When a statement and its converse are both true, combine them into a biconditional: \"p if and only if q.\""}]},{"say":"Deductive reasoning uses facts and rules to reach conclusions that must be true. It's how detectives and mathematicians think.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inductive reasoning finds patterns from examples (it might be wrong). Deductive reasoning uses accepted facts and logic, so its conclusions are guaranteed."},{"t":"list","items":["Law of Detachment: If p → q is true and p is true, then q is true.","Law of Syllogism: If p → q and q → r, then p → r."]},{"t":"p","text":"Properties of equality justify steps in proofs: addition, subtraction, multiplication and division properties; the substitution property; and the reflexive (a = a), symmetric (if a = b then b = a) and transitive (if a = b and b = c then a = c) properties."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Justify each step solving 2x + 5 = 17","steps":["2x = 12 by the Subtraction Property of Equality","x = 6 by the Division Property of Equality"],"answer":"x = 6, with a reason for each step"}]},{"say":"A proof is an argument where every step has a reason. Two-column proofs keep it neat and organized.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A two-column proof lists statements on the left and reasons on the right. Reasons can be given information, definitions, properties, postulates or theorems you've already proven."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Given ∠1 and ∠2 are a linear pair and m∠1 = 120°. Prove m∠2 = 60°.","steps":["∠1 and ∠2 are a linear pair (Given)","m∠1 + m∠2 = 180° (Linear pairs are supplementary)","120° + m∠2 = 180° (Substitution)","m∠2 = 60° (Subtraction Property of Equality)"],"answer":"Proven, with a reason for every statement"},{"t":"tip","text":"Start with the given information and work toward what you need to prove. It can help to work backward from the end, too."},{"t":"p","text":"A theorem is a statement that has been proven. The Vertical Angles Theorem says vertical angles are congruent; you can use it as a reason in later proofs."}]}],[{"say":"When a line cuts across two parallel lines, it creates angles that match up in predictable ways.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A transversal is a line that crosses two or more lines. It creates 8 angles. When the two lines are parallel:"},{"t":"list","items":["Corresponding angles (same position at each crossing) are congruent.","Alternate interior angles (inside, opposite sides) are congruent.","Alternate exterior angles (outside, opposite sides) are congruent.","Same-side (consecutive) interior angles are supplementary."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle is 65°. Find its same-side interior partner.","steps":["Same-side interior angles add to 180°","180° − 65°"],"answer":"115°"},{"t":"oops","text":"These angle rules only work when the lines are parallel. If they aren't, the angles don't have to match."}]},{"say":"The angle rules work backward too. If the angles match, the lines must be parallel!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The converses of the parallel line theorems let you prove lines are parallel:"},{"t":"list","items":["If corresponding angles are congruent, the lines are parallel.","If alternate interior (or alternate exterior) angles are congruent, the lines are parallel.","If same-side interior angles are supplementary, the lines are parallel."]},{"t":"p","text":"Two more facts: two lines parallel to the same line are parallel to each other, and two lines perpendicular to the same line (in a plane) are parallel."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A transversal makes angles of (4x − 8)° and (2x + 30)° as corresponding angles. What value of x makes the lines parallel?","steps":["Set them equal: 4x − 8 = 2x + 30","2x = 38"],"answer":"x = 19"}]},{"say":"On the coordinate plane, slope lets us check for parallel or perpendicular lines with simple arithmetic.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Parallel lines have equal slopes. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are opposite reciprocals (they multiply to −1)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Are the lines through (0, 1), (2, 5) and through (1, 0), (5, −2) perpendicular?","steps":["First slope: (5 − 1) ÷ (2 − 0) = 2","Second slope: (−2 − 0) ÷ (5 − 1) = −1/2","2 × (−1/2) = −1"],"answer":"Yes, they are perpendicular"},{"t":"p","text":"The distance from a point to a line is measured along the perpendicular segment, the shortest path."},{"t":"tip","text":"Vertical and horizontal lines are always perpendicular to each other."}]}],[{"say":"Transformations slide, flip and turn shapes. These three keep the shape exactly the same size.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rigid motions (isometries) preserve size and shape:"},{"t":"list","items":["Translation: slide every point the same distance and direction. (x, y) → (x + a, y + b).","Reflection: flip over a line. Over the x-axis: (x, y) → (x, −y). Over the y-axis: (x, y) → (−x, y). Over y = x: (x, y) → (y, x).","Rotation about the origin: 90° counterclockwise (x, y) → (−y, x); 180° (x, y) → (−x, −y); 270° counterclockwise (x, y) → (y, −x)."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Rotate the point (3, −2) 90° counterclockwise about the origin","steps":["Rule: (x, y) → (−y, x)","(−(−2), 3)"],"answer":"(2, 3)"},{"t":"p","text":"The original figure is the pre-image; the result is the image, often labeled with primes: A → A′."}]},{"say":"A dilation makes a shape bigger or smaller but keeps it the same shape. It's like zooming in or out.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A dilation with center at the origin and scale factor k maps (x, y) → (kx, ky)."},{"t":"list","items":["k > 1: enlargement. 0 < k < 1: reduction.","Dilations preserve angle measures and shape, but not size, so they are not rigid motions.","A dilated line segment is parallel to the original (or on the same line), and its length is multiplied by k."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Dilate triangle with vertices (2, 1), (4, 3), (0, 5) by a scale factor of 3","steps":["Multiply every coordinate by 3"],"answer":"(6, 3), (12, 9), (0, 15)"}]},{"say":"Two shapes are congruent if one can be moved onto the other with slides, flips and turns. Symmetry is a shape matching itself!","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Two figures are congruent if a sequence of rigid motions maps one onto the other. Congruent figures have all matching sides and angles equal."},{"t":"p","text":"A figure has line symmetry if a reflection over a line maps it onto itself. It has rotational symmetry if a rotation of less than 360° maps it onto itself."},{"t":"ex","problem":"How many lines of symmetry does a regular hexagon have, and what is its smallest rotation symmetry?","steps":["A regular n-gon has n lines of symmetry","Smallest rotation: 360° ÷ n = 360° ÷ 6"],"answer":"6 lines; 60° rotational symmetry"}]}],[{"say":"Every triangle's angles add to the same total. That one fact solves tons of problems.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The interior angles of a triangle always add to 180°."},{"t":"p","text":"The Exterior Angle Theorem: an exterior angle equals the sum of the two remote (non-adjacent) interior angles."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A triangle has angles of 48° and 67°. Find the third angle and the exterior angle next to it.","steps":["Third angle: 180° − 48° − 67° = 65°","Exterior angle: 48° + 67° = 115° (or 180° − 65°)"],"answer":"third angle 65°, exterior angle 115°"},{"t":"p","text":"Triangles can be classified by angles (acute, right, obtuse) or by sides (scalene: no equal sides, isosceles: at least two, equilateral: all three)."}]},{"say":"You don't need to check all six parts to know two triangles are congruent. Five shortcuts do the job!","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, and HL (for right triangles) prove triangles congruent. AAA and SSA do NOT."},{"t":"list","items":["SSS: all three pairs of sides are congruent.","SAS: two sides and the angle between them.","ASA: two angles and the side between them.","AAS: two angles and a side not between them.","HL: the hypotenuse and a leg of two right triangles."]},{"t":"p","text":"After proving triangles congruent, CPCTC (corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent) lets you conclude any matching parts are equal."},{"t":"oops","text":"The order of letters matters. In SAS, the angle must be between the two sides. \"SSA\" (angle not included) doesn't guarantee congruence."}]},{"say":"Triangles with equal sides have equal angles too. This link is super useful.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Isosceles Triangle Theorem: if two sides of a triangle are congruent, the angles opposite them are congruent. The converse is true too."},{"t":"p","text":"In an isosceles triangle, the two equal sides are legs, the third side is the base, and the angles next to the base are the base angles."},{"t":"p","text":"An equilateral triangle has three equal sides, so it also has three equal angles of 60° each."},{"t":"ex","problem":"An isosceles triangle has a vertex angle of 40°. Find each base angle.","steps":["The base angles are equal: x + x + 40 = 180","2x = 140"],"answer":"Each base angle is 70°"}]}],[{"say":"Special segments inside triangles have surprising properties. Let's explore a few.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A midsegment connects the midpoints of two sides. It is parallel to the third side and half as long."},{"t":"p","text":"A perpendicular bisector of a segment crosses it at its midpoint at a right angle. Any point on it is the same distance from both endpoints."},{"t":"p","text":"An angle bisector splits an angle in half. Any point on it is the same distance from both sides of the angle."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A midsegment of a triangle is 9 cm. How long is the side parallel to it?","steps":["The midsegment is half the third side","2 × 9"],"answer":"18 cm"}]},{"say":"Every triangle has four famous centers. Each one is where three special segments meet.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Each center is where three special segments are concurrent (meet at one point): perpendicular bisectors, angle bisectors, medians or altitudes."},{"t":"list","items":["Circumcenter: where the perpendicular bisectors meet. It's equidistant from the three vertices (center of the circle through them).","Incenter: where the angle bisectors meet. It's equidistant from the three sides (center of the circle inside).","Centroid: where the medians meet (a median goes from a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side). It's the balance point, located 2/3 of the way along each median from the vertex.","Orthocenter: where the altitudes meet."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A median is 15 units long. How far is the centroid from the vertex?","steps":["The centroid is 2/3 of the way from the vertex","2/3 × 15"],"answer":"10 units"}]},{"say":"Not just any three lengths can make a triangle! And bigger sides sit across from bigger angles.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Triangle Inequality Theorem: the sum of any two sides must be greater than the third side."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Can sides of 4, 7 and 12 form a triangle?","steps":["Check the two smallest: 4 + 7 = 11","11 is not greater than 12"],"answer":"No triangle"},{"t":"p","text":"In any triangle, the longest side is opposite the largest angle, and the shortest side is opposite the smallest angle."},{"t":"p","text":"If two sides are 5 and 9, the third side x must satisfy 9 − 5 < x < 9 + 5, so 4 < x < 14."}]}],[{"say":"How many degrees are inside a polygon? There's a simple formula that works for every one of them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The interior angles of an n-sided polygon add to (n − 2) × 180°. The exterior angles of any convex polygon add to 360°."},{"t":"p","text":"You can see why by drawing diagonals from one vertex: they split the polygon into n − 2 triangles, each with 180°."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find each interior angle of a regular octagon","steps":["Sum: (8 − 2) × 180° = 1080°","Each: 1080° ÷ 8"],"answer":"135°"},{"t":"p","text":"For a regular polygon, each exterior angle is 360° ÷ n. If each exterior angle is 24°, the polygon has 360 ÷ 24 = 15 sides."}]},{"say":"Parallelograms come with a whole bundle of properties. Know them, and lots of problems solve themselves.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel. In every parallelogram:"},{"t":"list","items":["Opposite sides are congruent.","Opposite angles are congruent.","Consecutive angles are supplementary.","The diagonals bisect each other."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"In parallelogram ABCD, angle A is 3x + 10 and angle B is 5x + 2. Find x and angle A.","steps":["Consecutive angles are supplementary: 3x + 10 + 5x + 2 = 180","8x + 12 = 180, so x = 21","angle A = 3(21) + 10"],"answer":"x = 21, angle A = 73°"},{"t":"p","text":"To prove a quadrilateral is a parallelogram, show any one of these: both pairs of opposite sides parallel or congruent, both pairs of opposite angles congruent, diagonals bisect each other, or one pair of sides both parallel and congruent."}]},{"say":"Rectangles, rhombuses and squares are special parallelograms, each with an extra superpower.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Rectangle: a parallelogram with four right angles. Its diagonals are congruent.","Rhombus: a parallelogram with four congruent sides. Its diagonals are perpendicular and bisect the angles.","Square: both a rectangle and a rhombus, so it has all of these properties.","Trapezoid: exactly one pair of parallel sides (the bases). Its midsegment is the average of the bases.","Isosceles trapezoid: legs congruent, base angles congruent, diagonals congruent.","Kite: two pairs of consecutive congruent sides; its diagonals are perpendicular."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A trapezoid has bases 10 and 18. How long is its midsegment?","steps":["Midsegment = (b₁ + b₂) ÷ 2","(10 + 18) ÷ 2"],"answer":"14"},{"t":"tip","text":"Think of it as a family tree: every square is a rhombus and a rectangle, and every rhombus and rectangle is a parallelogram. Properties pass down the tree."}]}],[{"say":"Similar figures are the same shape but different sizes, like a photo and its enlargement.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A proportion is an equation stating two ratios are equal. Solve it by cross-multiplying: if a/b = c/d, then ad = bc."},{"t":"p","text":"Two polygons are similar (~) if their corresponding angles are congruent and their corresponding sides are proportional. The ratio of matching sides is the scale factor."},{"t":"key","text":"If the scale factor is k, perimeters are multiplied by k and areas are multiplied by k²."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Two similar rectangles have widths 4 and 10. The small one has length 6. Find the large one's length.","steps":["4/10 = 6/x","4x = 60"],"answer":"x = 15"}]},{"say":"For triangles, you only need a couple of facts to prove they're similar. AA is the easiest!","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["AA: two pairs of congruent angles. (The third pair must match too.)","SSS similarity: all three pairs of sides are proportional.","SAS similarity: two pairs of sides are proportional and the included angles are congruent."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Triangle ABC has angles 40° and 75°. Triangle DEF has angles 75° and 65°. Are they similar?","steps":["ABC's third angle: 180° − 40° − 75° = 65°","Both have 75° and 65° angles"],"answer":"Yes, by AA similarity"},{"t":"tip","text":"Write similarity statements in matching order: if △ABC ~ △DEF, then A matches D, B matches E, and C matches F."}]},{"say":"Parallel lines inside triangles cut the sides into proportional pieces. It's a neat shortcut.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Triangle Proportionality Theorem: if a line parallel to one side of a triangle crosses the other two sides, it divides them proportionally."},{"t":"p","text":"The Angle Bisector Theorem: an angle bisector divides the opposite side into segments proportional to the other two sides."},{"t":"ex","problem":"In triangle ABC, DE ∥ BC with D on AB and E on AC. AD = 4, DB = 6, AE = 5. Find EC.","steps":["AD/DB = AE/EC","4/6 = 5/EC","4 · EC = 30"],"answer":"EC = 7.5"},{"t":"p","text":"Similar triangles also let you measure things you can't reach, like a tree's height using its shadow and your own shadow."}]}],[{"say":"The most famous theorem in math connects the three sides of every right triangle.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"In a right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c: a² + b² = c²."},{"t":"p","text":"The hypotenuse is the longest side, across from the right angle."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A ladder 13 ft long reaches 12 ft up a wall. How far is its base from the wall?","steps":["a² + 12² = 13²","a² = 169 − 144 = 25"],"answer":"a = 5 ft"},{"t":"p","text":"Pythagorean triples are whole-number sides that work: 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17, 7-24-25, and their multiples."},{"t":"p","text":"Converse: if a² + b² = c², the triangle is right. If a² + b² > c² it's acute; if a² + b² < c² it's obtuse (c is the longest side)."}]},{"say":"Two right triangles show up so often that their side ratios are worth memorizing.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"45-45-90 sides are x : x : x√2. 30-60-90 sides are x : x√3 : 2x."},{"t":"p","text":"In a 45-45-90 triangle (an isosceles right triangle), the hypotenuse is a leg times √2."},{"t":"p","text":"In a 30-60-90 triangle, the short leg is across from 30°, the long leg (across from 60°) is short leg × √3, and the hypotenuse is twice the short leg."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 30-60-90 triangle has a hypotenuse of 14. Find both legs.","steps":["Short leg = hypotenuse ÷ 2 = 7","Long leg = 7√3"],"answer":"7 and 7√3"},{"t":"tip","text":"A square's diagonal makes two 45-45-90 triangles, and an equilateral triangle's altitude makes two 30-60-90 triangles."}]},{"say":"Trigonometry links angles to side ratios. Just remember SOH-CAH-TOA!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For an acute angle in a right triangle:"},{"t":"key","text":"sin = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, cos = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, tan = opposite ÷ adjacent (SOH-CAH-TOA)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A ramp makes a 10° angle with the ground and is 20 ft long. How high does it rise?","steps":["The height is opposite the angle; 20 ft is the hypotenuse","sin 10° = h ÷ 20","h = 20 × sin 10° ≈ 20 × 0.174"],"answer":"about 3.5 ft"},{"t":"p","text":"To find an angle from sides, use the inverse functions: if tan A = 0.75, then A = tan⁻¹(0.75) ≈ 36.9°."},{"t":"p","text":"The angle of elevation is measured up from horizontal; the angle of depression is measured down from horizontal."}]}],[{"say":"Circles have their own vocabulary. Let's name the parts and learn how arcs are measured.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Radius: center to the circle. Diameter: across through the center (2 × radius).","Chord: a segment with both endpoints on the circle. The diameter is the longest chord.","Tangent: a line that touches the circle at exactly one point. It's perpendicular to the radius at that point.","Secant: a line that crosses the circle at two points."]},{"t":"p","text":"A central angle has its vertex at the center. The measure of its arc equals the central angle. A minor arc is less than 180°, a major arc is more, and a semicircle is exactly 180°."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A central angle measures 110°. What is the measure of its major arc?","steps":["The whole circle is 360°","360° − 110°"],"answer":"250°"}]},{"say":"An angle drawn from a point on the circle is half its arc. This half rule unlocks many circle problems.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"An inscribed angle is half the measure of its intercepted arc."},{"t":"p","text":"An inscribed angle has its vertex on the circle and its sides are chords. Inscribed angles that intercept the same arc are congruent."},{"t":"p","text":"An angle inscribed in a semicircle is always a right angle (because it intercepts a 180° arc)."},{"t":"p","text":"In a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle, opposite angles are supplementary."},{"t":"ex","problem":"An inscribed angle intercepts an arc of 140°. Find the angle.","steps":["Half the arc","140° ÷ 2"],"answer":"70°"}]},{"say":"To find part of a circle's length or area, just figure out what fraction of the circle you have.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Arc length = (angle ÷ 360°) × 2πr. Sector area = (angle ÷ 360°) × πr²."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A pizza has radius 8 in. Find the area of a 45° slice.","steps":["Fraction of the circle: 45/360 = 1/8","Whole area: π(8)² = 64π"],"answer":"8π ≈ 25.1 square inches"},{"t":"p","text":"The equation of a circle with center (h, k) and radius r is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²."},{"t":"oops","text":"In (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25, the center is (3, −2), not (3, 2), and the radius is 5, not 25."}]}],[{"say":"Area measures the space inside a flat shape. Each shape has a formula, and most come from the rectangle.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Rectangle: A = lw. Parallelogram: A = bh (h is the perpendicular height).","Triangle: A = ½bh.","Trapezoid: A = ½(b₁ + b₂)h.","Circle: A = πr².","Regular polygon: A = ½ × apothem × perimeter."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the area of a trapezoid with bases 7 and 13 and height 4","steps":["A = ½(7 + 13)(4)","½ × 20 × 4"],"answer":"40 square units"},{"t":"p","text":"For composite shapes, split into simpler shapes, find each area, and add (or subtract holes)."},{"t":"oops","text":"Height is always measured perpendicular to the base, not along a slanted side."}]},{"say":"Surface area is the total area covering a 3D shape, like the wrapping paper needed for a gift.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Surface area adds up the area of every face. Unfolding a solid into a flat net helps you see all the faces."},{"t":"list","items":["Rectangular prism: SA = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.","Cylinder: SA = 2πr² + 2πrh (two circles plus the rectangle that wraps around).","Cone: SA = πr² + πrℓ (ℓ is the slant height).","Sphere: SA = 4πr²."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the surface area of a cylinder with radius 3 and height 10","steps":["Two circles: 2π(3)² = 18π","Side: 2π(3)(10) = 60π"],"answer":"78π ≈ 245 square units"}]},{"say":"Volume measures how much space a solid takes up. Pointy shapes hold exactly one third of their matching prism!","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Prism and cylinder: V = Bh (B is the area of the base). Cylinder: V = πr²h.","Pyramid and cone: V = ⅓Bh. Cone: V = ⅓πr²h.","Sphere: V = (4/3)πr³."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A cone has radius 6 and height 10. Find its volume.","steps":["V = ⅓π(6)²(10)","⅓ × 36 × 10 = 120"],"answer":"120π ≈ 377 cubic units"},{"t":"p","text":"Cavalieri's Principle: if two solids have the same height and the same cross-section area at every level, they have the same volume."},{"t":"p","text":"If a solid is scaled by a factor k, its volume is multiplied by k³."}]}]];
export default text;
